#edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75a10b66-5a8f-5871-9339-219b0e3e74d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447954} {:answer/id #uuid "f07c08ea-4ded-5969-9a0d-3e0fe297bfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a10b66-5a8f-5871-9339-219b0e3e74d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447954} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefce898-b060-52ac-83be-8cd47e2e1931" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fa81e71-e4f5-5405-bfdd-41cd7387b276" :question/math-academy-id "q-108044" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\n- **II.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/title "Mathematics for Machine Learning: Placement Exam" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045462 :db/ident :activity.type/diagnostic} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419031} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419075} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/title "Mathematics for Machine Learning: Placement Exam" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045462 :db/ident :activity.type/diagnostic} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557865} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558687}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph" :topic/_kn