ec2f92" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b86aaf3-f04f-5a9d-8491-875b7ee69bad" :question/math-academy-id "q-108605" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6e311063c3222d8be1b5fe37379d21c4dbb3198afd7c338a273bb1cf366fc1e.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph II shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049731 :topic/id #uuid "0f127f4a-3cf5-5736-90b8-9817b73eb050" :topic/math-academy-id 1889 :topic/title "Invertible Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7979d09-acc1-57e7-ad97-48330dcf92c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437534} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb2c6be-faa3-57d6-b152-c27d364876ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437535}] :answer-field/correc