/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a35e1aa3-c186-545a-9105-317f752c0d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-109517" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-10I:$\n\n$$\nA-10I=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-10I:$\n\n$$\n(A-10I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-10I\\,\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-10I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =10$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a25c4edf-bc28-5a6d-bce8-dc691f40f46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461575} {:answer/id #uuid "b25784ef-0104-536a-b0f5-9202aac0e151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a25c4edf-bc28-5a6d-bce8-dc691f40f46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461575} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1cae5fa-1b6c-55f0-b295-ad6fed3c38ae" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462541} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858d8067-b0de-598a-b606-236eb811c558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461577} {:answer/id #uuid "4852a013-cee8-55ea-a31e-65f55bad1029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4852a013-cee8-55ea-a31e-65f55bad1029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461578} :answer-field/id #uuid "35ccb795-d8ad-55c8-a867-52adde8a804d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462542} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6f3d6d1-2d3e-5256-8645-40f5fb25a5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461579} {:answer/id #uuid "ef647bd5-b2da-51b9-8380-c98b65a2a02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6f3d6d1-2d3e-5256-8645-40f5fb25a5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461579} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed24b2ea-7eb1-5ee6-99ae-1a38c15df738" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a65ad08d-f6cf-551f-aa17-5ecb903998d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109524" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"} :activity/title "Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481770} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482639}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc