/ref 17592186461652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f70460d9-df3a-58dd-95a2-bafff6dc83d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461651} :answer-field/id #uuid "18092fb5-74d5-5ff7-870d-5e5eab99935e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d55fbe8-7170-5840-8a9a-b1f2a55c58fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-110611" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n- **I.** $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xyz=1}$\n- **II.** $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x+y-z=0}$\n- **III.** $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:y=-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $xyz=1$ since $0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x+y-z=0$ since $0+0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ba35c72-4811-55d0-b2b1-fb8116a2cc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461653} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2a422a-84a1-5671-bc55-3b45224e58cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ba35c72-4811-55d0-b2b1-fb8116a2cc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461653} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0888cfa-5fff-5bcc-9c6f-d3cf0a398b99" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2974aa62-0b1f-5343-a4a5-c39a1584c078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461655} {:answer/id #uuid "78c8ebf0-1d54-5046-ae1c-3426546d3565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78c8ebf0-1d54-5046-ae1c-3426546d3565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461656} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad91d256-6b2d-51b9-8149-c46bca013503" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462581} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5784de9d-74b4-5c34-9c61-2b2f1a7172ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461657} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa10535-80e6-5c1d-a5e9-5bed7b7ca613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5784de9d-74b4-5c34-9c61-2b2f1a7172ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461657} :answer-field/id #uuid "822bc400-c192-58d1-80df-f14e9a428a6a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49bd226a-4547-5889-a62c-e6c2912a1520" :question/math-academy-id "q-110760" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each subset.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. For instance, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x-4y=1$ since $0-4\\cdot 0=0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=-2y$ since $0=-2\\cdot 0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. No point in ${R}^{3}$ satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0.$ Hence, $C=∅.$ The empty set is not a subspace since it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"} :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434588} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434599}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6d33351-e47b-5dcf-8a4d-d3e61f058c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461659} {:answer/id #uuid "d95202b2-7ac0-51a9-beba-433590fd067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d33351-e47b-5dcf-8a4d-d3e61f058c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461659} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7a303b-9974-55fa-9193-b45a893b05b7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462583} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c0fe0f-a14e-5a97-bb81-d2b618017a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461661} {:answer/id #uuid "52cb8444-2346-56eb-8483-17ca5a3815c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52cb8444-2346-56eb-8483-17ca5a3815c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461662} :answer-field/id #uuid "df43bab4-a790-5959-8eef-b12b9e48205b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462584} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c438405c-079c-5cda-acfc-5fc8f4718e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461663} {:answer/id #uuid "688e99c6-1ace-56a4-a978-0e19e70df677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c438405c-079c-5cda-acfc-5fc8f4718e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461663} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fcae7ac-a05d-5215-8944-c0e4000c37c6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc11ea3b-3ee9-5cb9-a26d-3aab9cb82498" :question/math-academy-id "q-110761" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n- **I.** $A={⟨2t,2+t⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R}$\n- **II.** $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$\n- **III.** $C={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have, for some $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 2+t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t &=0 \\\\ 2+t &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace.  It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $y=1$ since $0\\ne 1.$ So, $0$ is not in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755 :topic/id #uuid "1125135d-4341-5e5d-9065-381cc0460203" :topic/math-academy-id 942 :topic/title "Closure Properties of Polynomials"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4297f89-fd28-59d0-8a01-1af1a32b248a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447992} {:answer/id #uuid "66b18ac9-a067-5e9e-bebd-cda7eba3504b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66b18ac9-a067-5e9e-bebd-cda7eba3504b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447993} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e83c0a-ba45-5a06-aff2-f4a09ceb7761" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504aaa69-9711-59c3-a3b5-ab54642bafb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447994} {:answer/id #uuid "9524d92b-ef29-5e55-9a7b-fe70c47c1538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504aaa69-9711-59c3-a3b5-ab54642bafb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447994} :answer-field/id #uuid "062e4517-2a59-51d0-9e87-12b76ecd0afe" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f9d306f-8ce1-56ae-a6c6-235700e213ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447996} {:answer/id #uuid "17477713-8cd0-55aa-a429-b3b2a613cd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f9d306f-8ce1-56ae-a6c6-235700e213ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447996} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb69639-775d-5bc7-808b-b95d936c7455" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc384b99-060e-5785-8885-6518d3e3cae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-111385" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- **III.** $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755 :topic/id #uuid "1125135d-4341-5e5d-9065-381cc0460203" :topic/math-academy-id 942 :topic/title "Closure Properties of Polynomials"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc8055b-41ba-5afd-a4b7-8f5ae98c1bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447998} {:answer/id #uuid "713dda88-c35d-5b62-ba3e-3a7c5c9158ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "713dda88-c35d-5b62-ba3e-3a7c5c9158ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447999} :answer-field/id #uuid "0158263e-6b5a-5d03-b569-19c98b4ea4f9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a60c243-874b-5e7d-a9de-205150ddf19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448000} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3c6957-3df1-556b-a8a4-d04a20eb8b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd3c6957-3df1-556b-a8a4-d04a20eb8b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448001} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f10c59e-096d-53c3-b35b-f56e4b0a76cc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da3dc2a6-e40a-5ffd-bd17-d85ee2cefe2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448002} {:answer/id #uuid "9401d805-a8db-578d-8812-e1292cd7e0f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da3dc2a6-e40a-5ffd-bd17-d85ee2cefe2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448002} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b462fe-7ba1-5816-9b5d-0503d1cb0a7d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d80a52b-66a2-53ce-b5ad-ecebb8222def" :question/math-academy-id "q-111390" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- **III.** $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050046 :topic/id #uuid "288ef1bc-9e03-59cc-957a-e1edeebef5c3" :topic/math-academy-id 2050 :topic/title "End Behavior of Polynomials"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6faebac8-ebda-5f28-9ae5-f47ea62ba4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461665} {:answer/id #uuid "89f5755d-41a2-5951-8169-455f39e871ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f5755d-41a2-5951-8169-455f39e871ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461666} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f6caba-9c1c-5b6e-b1e1-a94766e26a33" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462586} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a776ef48-bad8-5973-b3d2-424cd31a8efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461667} {:answer/id #uuid "03cb7ee7-1cb7-5269-9d2c-bcb3d98685e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a776ef48-bad8-5973-b3d2-424cd31a8efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461667} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94d55a2-ccc3-50de-94dc-85a31579f29d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462587} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71e6b7db-27af-50cb-9f24-3efd638462db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461669} {:answer/id #uuid "638eb8bc-d919-5679-86a7-346c5b27b82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "638eb8bc-d919-5679-86a7-346c5b27b82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461670} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b59df70-0a5b-5c66-b162-e3a8bbcf21c0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57caf7d5-ab2c-51c1-a72f-c7af8b4259a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-112114" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b46093feaf5d7138e30c6d6fc673a2d915cac74a23ae76e0841e9bb5b135fa60.png)\n\nThe graph of the polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The degree of $P(x)$ is even.\n- The coefficient of the leading term is positive.\n- The constant term is positive.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo, statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is positive, and therefore the constant term must be positive. So, III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115300 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68350e0d-84ca-5b50-b05a-1bca3ceea40f" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 3x3 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049684 :topic/id #uuid "0aa448d3-ff23-589f-85d0-370c4ca9baa8" :topic/math-academy-id 1732 :topic/title "Finding the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using Row Operations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f56d4881-7927-5529-b7ce-6e102c93f448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461671} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9dce1c-b833-5c08-93d1-0c74dc942a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f56d4881-7927-5529-b7ce-6e102c93f448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461671} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cde7f3-5f67-5b5b-9c7a-6d7bf978beb9" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee9a4a5-d913-56a8-83f1-b7f62e66a405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461673} {:answer/id #uuid "86853822-71a9-5acb-bb33-07c7ce5b8214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86853822-71a9-5acb-bb33-07c7ce5b8214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461674} :answer-field/id #uuid "2999816c-8908-5862-9060-a3312fc6213c" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462590} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac75fabb-620c-5750-b221-3da182d40988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461675} {:answer/id #uuid "ead1030f-902a-5380-8016-bb1c25d5fc02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac75fabb-620c-5750-b221-3da182d40988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461675} :answer-field/id #uuid "257d0ac2-eb5b-5cc0-ac28-84a63abdb8df" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8563367f-0e91-5d7d-a84d-c182442533d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-112249" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738 :topic/title "Generalized Eigenvectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c4aa29-25a2-5420-aaaa-474634177ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448004} {:answer/id #uuid "6239020d-aaf4-5fe4-a820-c620ab71e99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c4aa29-25a2-5420-aaaa-474634177ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448004} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef24bf57-2f01-5432-9ac4-0d48397c5756" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449042} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f95c68-b61f-5133-b49d-6adac9b4640c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448006} {:answer/id #uuid "1be585ce-f404-5d77-bc35-25a343b2c635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f95c68-b61f-5133-b49d-6adac9b4640c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448006} :answer-field/id #uuid "b996260e-b2d2-5f6f-a512-3f34a15eb2bf" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d65f7c26-d93c-564a-b50c-12755d1b153e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448008} {:answer/id #uuid "758f8f50-5658-5025-8870-c168848da070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "758f8f50-5658-5025-8870-c168848da070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448009} :answer-field/id #uuid "baeae7a3-137e-50fe-b6e4-63f32aa365c4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeced2e5-384e-5308-b21f-d88eb6c697e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-112252" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115300 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68350e0d-84ca-5b50-b05a-1bca3ceea40f" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 3x3 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049684 :topic/id #uuid "0aa448d3-ff23-589f-85d0-370c4ca9baa8" :topic/math-academy-id 1732 :topic/title "Finding the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using Row Operations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f9beaf-0424-5aa2-956d-c07c9eb62b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461677} {:answer/id #uuid "66a00ca0-0b71-5677-9842-8f027893c915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37f9beaf-0424-5aa2-956d-c07c9eb62b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461677} :answer-field/id #uuid "017cb54c-3b6b-50b1-95d4-f81dad5ee6be" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462592} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8c85ad-fd15-5dd4-9e14-bfd98d611ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461679} {:answer/id #uuid "622396aa-29f0-5139-afe4-e49c5d3f0ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "622396aa-29f0-5139-afe4-e49c5d3f0ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461680} :answer-field/id #uuid "c12b488e-8d41-5b9e-986a-be677815ba9a" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2d91a2-2e88-5c9f-9d61-48ce06535522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461681} {:answer/id #uuid "eef41a24-0089-5457-9ffa-94b6b8d94abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d2d91a2-2e88-5c9f-9d61-48ce06535522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461681} :answer-field/id #uuid "33aba9a9-bfdf-5094-ac86-ede37e0f6247" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28f1bc51-adb8-5a90-9cf3-77945badfc4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112379" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -28 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -14 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050601 :topic/id #uuid "577aebce-a9af-5bcd-867f-fb32f559c852" :topic/math-academy-id 2049 :topic/title "Graphing General Polynomials"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe7b4219-7bd9-5af7-9497-e72b402b4c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437546} {:answer/id #uuid "564c2782-36b7-5050-95c3-ecc6b4397067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe7b4219-7bd9-5af7-9497-e72b402b4c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437546} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf89ac3-0f0b-5e9c-b0e8-3cc03db964db" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438802} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c28f8c9-90a7-51fe-9930-a6da19f2257d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437548} {:answer/id #uuid "11ae88b8-4ffa-5701-b226-080d00b79940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11ae88b8-4ffa-5701-b226-080d00b79940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437549} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba202f57-81ba-5b7d-90b4-e84c309c4a75" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e827f74-38ef-5ab5-8362-7e32213026b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437550} {:answer/id #uuid "be765630-4d1d-5deb-953a-819ff4c18894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be765630-4d1d-5deb-953a-819ff4c18894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437551} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bcc424-fa6d-5745-a593-c71006c1d5a3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71e1e299-84ff-5a79-94c6-c0078815dc95" :question/math-academy-id "q-112585" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e4e2241490626139e15800f3b90c8e15a9963bdcace9907f8557b0897d533e6.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $p(0)$ is positive.\n- **II.** $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n- **III.** $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph passes through the $y$-axis at $y=0,$ which means that $p(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050601 :topic/id #uuid "577aebce-a9af-5bcd-867f-fb32f559c852" :topic/math-academy-id 2049 :topic/title "Graphing General Polynomials"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6ed7dd8-66bc-5071-a37b-951be908d6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437552} {:answer/id #uuid "704578a1-0e4a-595b-92eb-eb054da1a896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6ed7dd8-66bc-5071-a37b-951be908d6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437552} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f102cd-f012-58fa-bb1e-aaba22574b2c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438805} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ae4065-0902-5d39-8e60-d94c4d044941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437554} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e1e703-3563-57e8-a33b-c4d461d13f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4e1e703-3563-57e8-a33b-c4d461d13f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437555} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb599a0-7b6d-5eda-b1cc-1490ff53bccb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc7e2bcb-12bd-5d36-961b-0822cf128846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437556} {:answer/id #uuid "007486f7-f7cb-5355-b8c3-7905fd60e0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc7e2bcb-12bd-5d36-961b-0822cf128846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437556} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fc1b00-0e36-5e62-acd3-5be7dc024152" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "576d8616-a0cd-59d9-a961-712779c4c308" :question/math-academy-id "q-112587" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa431232a1f9ef2374f4e8a9c0202cf60ce1dcfef69c2a6fca604fe6aed7b681.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $p(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n- **II.** $p(0)=0.$\n- **III.** $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph passes through the $y$-axis at $y=0$, which means that $p(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049363 :topic/id #uuid "eefd5285-6b99-569d-a5bb-365f176fa757" :topic/math-academy-id 807 :topic/title "Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70fe01b0-227a-5299-a161-e614268fbbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439423} {:answer/id #uuid "19923d61-aaa9-54ba-9bc8-e75850766907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70fe01b0-227a-5299-a161-e614268fbbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439423} :answer-field/id #uuid "3903db53-ae00-5b1c-8e33-32b047656dc9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a930356d-6d4c-569f-b12a-b386d7729af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439425} {:answer/id #uuid "f2898ee2-4e9c-5934-bab6-9a4519d02031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a930356d-6d4c-569f-b12a-b386d7729af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439425} :answer-field/id #uuid "983e2156-7634-56e9-b6ed-ce20bc2e7fd6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d35753e-3243-5400-90c2-494ad0ea42cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439427} {:answer/id #uuid "83e3869a-d37e-5b05-b347-568de2c06346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e3869a-d37e-5b05-b347-568de2c06346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439428} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac7622c-6ae1-5c5e-a9ea-df5796734d8f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c538ce5-ccb5-5dd4-83dc-819839f73fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439429} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc375ff-fae1-5c3f-a326-bc262ba269ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c538ce5-ccb5-5dd4-83dc-819839f73fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439429} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdeb008-c261-5a02-bdfb-70133c5efaf5" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "163eeae3-71ad-5b66-a0aa-4b05d346c12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113525" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-7$\n- **II.** $x=-1$\n- **III.** $x=0$\n- **IV.** $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x({x}^{2}+x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+7)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+7=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(7) \\\\  & =1-28 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049363 :topic/id #uuid "eefd5285-6b99-569d-a5bb-365f176fa757" :topic/math-academy-id 807 :topic/title "Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb9d7b25-5854-520d-8ebf-a6801866a103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439439} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd9b49f-1e1e-558e-9235-e08d3e02c10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d7b25-5854-520d-8ebf-a6801866a103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439439} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9087893-bc60-52fc-8d81-3cedf3411ac6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ddf72a-287c-5883-9635-ec2b9399d667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439441} {:answer/id #uuid "59344e22-da7e-5c4e-9b1f-2a9bd6abb444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ddf72a-287c-5883-9635-ec2b9399d667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439441} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46f02c4-4697-588e-bc3e-a16a5e0bec49" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044decdf-112a-5933-b2b2-02be3db90f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439443} {:answer/id #uuid "91341243-61ec-5cbb-8cb0-aa4b45e28585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "044decdf-112a-5933-b2b2-02be3db90f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439443} :answer-field/id #uuid "f27daf57-7ce5-534a-ba37-ad6313ed2cc9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daec1f65-9c08-5df4-939b-bebb2395a749" :question/math-academy-id "q-113545" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-1$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =1-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478 :topic/id #uuid "a2bb3232-296a-5d16-b145-1ee30beae8b3" :topic/math-academy-id 1360 :topic/title "Using the First Derivative Test to Classify Local Extrema"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32a048d-f2cb-54f2-9d1c-d5c3468e04c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448010} {:answer/id #uuid "b7098d04-def7-5d1a-80e5-e99f3272049a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a32a048d-f2cb-54f2-9d1c-d5c3468e04c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448010} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5bb018e-2a62-5571-a516-050637012e4e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa8115eb-6e91-5df7-bcac-d93c78c8f383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448012} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9cca76-a57f-55b6-ac71-e3048ca44dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb9cca76-a57f-55b6-ac71-e3048ca44dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448013} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b656c7d-4a13-5813-aa66-e861fb5ded55" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ebebaf5-a6d7-594e-b367-839ab189ccb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448014} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ef1912-45da-5352-b875-d5361ff285e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9ef1912-45da-5352-b875-d5361ff285e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448015} :answer-field/id #uuid "c08fa681-305e-5e38-af33-ece01598330e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4af7007e-9d89-5ab4-8e7e-891076eaef01" :question/math-academy-id "q-113756" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- **II.** $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- **III.** $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-2x \\\\  & =2x(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x-1)\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,\\frac{1}{4},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)-1)=8>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =2(\\frac{1}{4})[3(\\frac{1}{4})-1]=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)-1)=4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478 :topic/id #uuid "a2bb3232-296a-5d16-b145-1ee30beae8b3" :topic/math-academy-id 1360 :topic/title "Using the First Derivative Test to Classify Local Extrema"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4141d585-9a26-5c05-b314-a502e6c516a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448016} {:answer/id #uuid "505582ef-1aef-57a8-9ce7-4d38dfdd5704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "505582ef-1aef-57a8-9ce7-4d38dfdd5704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448017} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2bc770-f435-5bd8-99a3-74a5171df35e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb11f7ca-fbfa-5dbc-ba7c-6491a4d66a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448018} {:answer/id #uuid "b5658152-f15d-5505-8c77-5717f8e0daf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5658152-f15d-5505-8c77-5717f8e0daf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b638333-bcb5-5fb3-b3f9-bbefccae69b1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0702bcf1-cf08-5269-b943-98a8b5eab715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448020} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc66a4-8679-5e2c-b09f-62638a0f8fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0702bcf1-cf08-5269-b943-98a8b5eab715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448020} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c5cf9c-9a56-5bd8-9808-1b05b4dbfab2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04886f18-ebcf-50be-b62d-6d531bb447d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-113758" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- **II.** $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- **III.** $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050120 :topic/id #uuid "2e9764be-8ae2-5f34-ba40-eb17adf36f47" :topic/math-academy-id 3846 :topic/title "Relating Concavity to the Second Derivative"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2bbc1d9-5734-59e8-8fc8-7b6c674c67b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448022} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3ae338-b812-5c8a-824a-e79b401f8d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bbc1d9-5734-59e8-8fc8-7b6c674c67b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448022} :answer-field/id #uuid "752de745-0621-5409-947c-19362f0dd84f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c0206e-5daa-5aab-bd99-aa60e11d2422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448024} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d5652c-9bc4-593a-82a1-a0d3968f2e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d5652c-9bc4-593a-82a1-a0d3968f2e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448025} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae3e41d-7d96-5607-aa7e-628fc697cf0e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b853d92c-2288-5bf6-b37e-e337642d9dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448026} {:answer/id #uuid "f9456f02-4552-5b67-8c10-0c7758cb593d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b853d92c-2288-5bf6-b37e-e337642d9dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448026} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c28a799-a132-5f6e-a3b8-d8bf0c6a857b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aab077f8-99df-5fb1-8e2c-0476334114c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114270" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](images/09/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050120 :topic/id #uuid "2e9764be-8ae2-5f34-ba40-eb17adf36f47" :topic/math-academy-id 3846 :topic/title "Relating Concavity to the Second Derivative"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16429656-5c03-5aa3-aaf3-aa7d1ebeed61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461683} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c0d93b-dc86-5e59-8c16-ccc5da156329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16429656-5c03-5aa3-aaf3-aa7d1ebeed61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461683} :answer-field/id #uuid "c796127d-850a-53b5-b48c-1f585671fed2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462595} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f3546f-2758-5f53-a269-4d4aac9e177f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461685} {:answer/id #uuid "4df5b965-5823-584a-8079-1c5d3f84b3e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12f3546f-2758-5f53-a269-4d4aac9e177f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461685} :answer-field/id #uuid "df94b234-868c-5886-8226-2fcb1ab760d4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1143cfad-0316-51bf-9cdb-844d953db58a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461687} {:answer/id #uuid "e27044c7-2a0f-537d-a826-3026abb1ea25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27044c7-2a0f-537d-a826-3026abb1ea25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461688} :answer-field/id #uuid "702ba749-e898-51df-b2ab-8e3eb37a36d7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a2a1a5b-82e0-576b-9a2b-9cca3c9ca19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114271" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050120 :topic/id #uuid "2e9764be-8ae2-5f34-ba40-eb17adf36f47" :topic/math-academy-id 3846 :topic/title "Relating Concavity to the Second Derivative"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7968f616-b6cd-5912-9ebb-c7e943c53ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461689} {:answer/id #uuid "315f2abe-9a26-53e9-83fa-b2133c290710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315f2abe-9a26-53e9-83fa-b2133c290710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461690} :answer-field/id #uuid "796a9a88-fbec-5d78-a6c2-5887f0b84dee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462598} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0501edb0-6193-5111-9965-3cf69fd6d025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461691} {:answer/id #uuid "2aaefe3a-0e2c-598d-8ca5-0122976d169d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0501edb0-6193-5111-9965-3cf69fd6d025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461691} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfbfcd2b-c257-521a-a3e3-88fac2fbb5fa" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462599} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf0cb466-895c-55a0-a52e-cf20a384c530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461693} {:answer/id #uuid "70393936-4fa8-59bd-bb3e-4252b95fbf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf0cb466-895c-55a0-a52e-cf20a384c530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461693} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9be2509-66d6-56fb-8f54-59b1e96de6a5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "136bbef5-7f5b-5272-816c-ea16bb2ceed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-114272" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+(2x-2){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for all real values $x\\ne 0,$ the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ So $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{x}<0,$ which does not have solutions. So $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050120 :topic/id #uuid "2e9764be-8ae2-5f34-ba40-eb17adf36f47" :topic/math-academy-id 3846 :topic/title "Relating Concavity to the Second Derivative"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c61788c-aa38-569e-b064-a62aa1fafa38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448028} {:answer/id #uuid "962a8718-7882-50c5-b220-0741c4360d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "962a8718-7882-50c5-b220-0741c4360d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448029} :answer-field/id #uuid "10778036-82f9-5f0b-9feb-b869e392a286" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fe543c-c4a0-5406-80b2-7ad2f17bf1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448030} {:answer/id #uuid "d977bd44-f4ea-59f9-ba62-6f8c0a48c249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00fe543c-c4a0-5406-80b2-7ad2f17bf1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448030} :answer-field/id #uuid "3428e20e-db55-5b2c-bf82-9ce446c21c80" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4660e71-9cac-5faf-999e-f585c344a5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448032} {:answer/id #uuid "fa091b57-a8d7-575e-9b8b-b0eb7da3ee75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa091b57-a8d7-575e-9b8b-b0eb7da3ee75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448033} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa1c36a7-587d-5b7e-874c-0f0edb11faa8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e263520-c83b-578e-886f-bcaf93059761" :question/math-academy-id "q-114273" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,e)$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,e)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(e,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x \\\\  & =2x(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =8-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & <1 \\\\ x & <e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on  $(0,e).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)<0,$ which has the solution $(e,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050120 :topic/id #uuid "2e9764be-8ae2-5f34-ba40-eb17adf36f47" :topic/math-academy-id 3846 :topic/title "Relating Concavity to the Second Derivative"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242f19c6-0790-5b73-a95d-b9bf3ce752ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461695} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fad236-c416-5ae4-a529-59fa15587eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "242f19c6-0790-5b73-a95d-b9bf3ce752ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461695} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ff58d7-6b13-59d9-8094-7aa224dde724" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acf973ce-31c7-59e8-8863-2afd23e29694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461697} {:answer/id #uuid "09c9c565-acb1-5e93-8cf2-d8560f1ed564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c9c565-acb1-5e93-8cf2-d8560f1ed564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461698} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0be23d3-289f-5073-8701-6cd532ad237d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "600c592f-d550-5ca3-9317-e6c0c5c0e4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461699} {:answer/id #uuid "9d046dd3-43dc-5a20-9121-50f2c2cf750b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "600c592f-d550-5ca3-9317-e6c0c5c0e4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461699} :answer-field/id #uuid "884c486d-26aa-5b2b-a395-c1a37b40db8d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1339af8-7ec4-512f-92c7-059b9ab8ffee" :question/math-academy-id "q-114277" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883 :topic/id #uuid "c96abbae-0759-5f81-9064-a4b37d0b7422" :topic/math-academy-id 339 :topic/title "The Second Derivative Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a72430ec-f6b3-5a9f-9bc6-24032ddfc234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461701} {:answer/id #uuid "263a2b10-5369-5daa-a857-d4f1a56ed0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "263a2b10-5369-5daa-a857-d4f1a56ed0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461702} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d3b3d6-f7d5-5785-b33f-2e2d8b7829df" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ad5dd7-be3c-56db-a7bc-0110b4655953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461703} {:answer/id #uuid "bc70f5c3-d6e5-5e0a-bff8-3c04c94eee40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc70f5c3-d6e5-5e0a-bff8-3c04c94eee40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461704} :answer-field/id #uuid "dba2832e-7fc5-54f6-bacd-ebd504e6a597" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462605} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e91a09-6937-50af-a536-8c4dbce2fd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461705} {:answer/id #uuid "211188c2-8354-500b-b2dc-9d9d6b844de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37e91a09-6937-50af-a536-8c4dbce2fd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461705} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6ff90e-2bbf-5a1d-9cb9-ebcc273dbe85" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a75ae1b4-9111-5369-af8d-34d27de2f589" :question/math-academy-id "q-114414" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- **II.** ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=0$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(0)=-1<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(0)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=0\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883 :topic/id #uuid "c96abbae-0759-5f81-9064-a4b37d0b7422" :topic/math-academy-id 339 :topic/title "The Second Derivative Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a4d35c-0841-541b-b560-2c04eab07774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448034} {:answer/id #uuid "576ed81d-346d-54a5-bdcd-390cabb1b99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576ed81d-346d-54a5-bdcd-390cabb1b99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448035} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a7afcf-d257-585a-8030-5f74873574f6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b78632-1e83-53ea-8dec-39f48d7a699d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448036} {:answer/id #uuid "25222a5b-fda0-5cb7-8372-30db249ebf36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25222a5b-fda0-5cb7-8372-30db249ebf36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448037} :answer-field/id #uuid "4add0ed0-b880-509f-898a-08124b4ba8d3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0154929c-b7d9-56cf-8571-78bb55fecdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448038} {:answer/id #uuid "e329db6c-2dcf-58dd-aa26-72c5775c72f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0154929c-b7d9-56cf-8571-78bb55fecdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448038} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f7fb03-5a8f-5375-a386-673f2d505b72" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80d2d71a-65f2-56a2-b3b6-ec354b205e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448040} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa60caf-ba86-5af1-a00b-444fcc5dc068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fa60caf-ba86-5af1-a00b-444fcc5dc068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448041} :answer-field/id #uuid "95423d4a-b210-5868-b668-c08c1ff0e483" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b90d338d-3db1-5cee-ac01-ae8e8b265add" :question/math-academy-id "q-114415" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- **II.** ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- **IV.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083303 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1894719c-d398-515d-9a7b-6af74727106c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Improper Integrals of the Second Kind" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050379 :topic/id #uuid "445c7063-c967-5f21-b424-ab8820e310ca" :topic/math-academy-id 759 :topic/title "Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4b326e-ef6b-5d21-ab1a-ca7a75adb011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461707} {:answer/id #uuid "4a23ae88-cb6f-5067-9e7c-78e3e5de0677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b4b326e-ef6b-5d21-ab1a-ca7a75adb011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461707} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ff2055-5c8d-508d-9e6d-467f3c7ea5b7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42fcda12-e6db-5458-b933-a286041a0120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461709} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccbe075-1b58-5a51-956c-750dd28874a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42fcda12-e6db-5458-b933-a286041a0120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461709} :answer-field/id #uuid "36eb85be-0609-5ea8-bc5d-87f681a2e2d4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d4bac5-8f20-51e1-9155-b1aaa06d3df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461711} {:answer/id #uuid "b872e627-a1b3-5c84-b92b-1ffa7cb5df6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b872e627-a1b3-5c84-b92b-1ffa7cb5df6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461712} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31909ac-e324-5221-aa66-cd5831ba14dc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a47dc9e-71f1-5fb5-bcb1-b46747ed60f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115206" :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975 :topic/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a720bb2-bbc4-573e-8407-a82a7cd6aced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461713} {:answer/id #uuid "bc269fcb-4729-553f-9e8e-37462bb475e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc269fcb-4729-553f-9e8e-37462bb475e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461714} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd44e88c-679d-5889-9504-27652463140d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462610} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e19dc2e2-8cd3-586b-b1da-633c599ce417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461715} {:answer/id #uuid "6926fd17-3961-5e39-b0e6-1d90c73a427f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6926fd17-3961-5e39-b0e6-1d90c73a427f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461716} :answer-field/id #uuid "46e92563-432d-59bb-b28f-0665e826c23d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d1d7f3-dfbd-5733-a4de-559bc6b21d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461717} {:answer/id #uuid "3342d6ad-aba5-5f6e-a12c-c253657151d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08d1d7f3-dfbd-5733-a4de-559bc6b21d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461717} :answer-field/id #uuid "5509a19e-72b1-5c27-957c-d0bce5b07490" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05da4bb4-efa5-5a74-9032-2827a097e82b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115263" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975 :topic/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a841c83-c0e9-5548-b814-ce0a3ffb2d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461719} {:answer/id #uuid "2e47707c-e2a1-5139-bc7e-e581dc66eb9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a841c83-c0e9-5548-b814-ce0a3ffb2d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461719} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc3e7cd3-ca1a-58c4-9300-8f544b1abaec" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462613} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9b9bc7-befc-5680-8c8c-18aa54ebb0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461721} {:answer/id #uuid "77cf71b4-bf47-50db-9251-b4e0e740d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77cf71b4-bf47-50db-9251-b4e0e740d066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461722} :answer-field/id #uuid "e594767d-c8a4-5f3c-b4d7-646f2605402e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462614} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eecadbbf-c020-5e7f-900a-560bd7259f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461723} {:answer/id #uuid "a36b0bc7-28e6-5d80-978c-9eaa714d757f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a36b0bc7-28e6-5d80-978c-9eaa714d757f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461724} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ab1077-5e87-549f-9136-2d3a0ac9fbe4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c52ca68f-33c1-5cf6-9bde-bf78ae92f92f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115275" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{5}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral I is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975 :topic/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0685885c-7c90-5223-9e10-8c472091afff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461725} {:answer/id #uuid "518f0912-92a4-5597-9f9b-9be330ce54ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "518f0912-92a4-5597-9f9b-9be330ce54ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461726} :answer-field/id #uuid "c202cb15-dcf0-5a32-b9ea-37b8fb7672e9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462616} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ec0c3d2-ece4-52ce-986b-b7cf55839ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461727} {:answer/id #uuid "e3800ee8-1c0b-5a22-9376-d173c968665d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec0c3d2-ece4-52ce-986b-b7cf55839ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461727} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c180afb-a843-503a-bf9f-47686e1fee3e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462617} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272413a5-104c-59d8-89b6-750264b534ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461729} {:answer/id #uuid "4e07e286-0bdd-5fd8-89f8-1b04c3d5d2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e07e286-0bdd-5fd8-89f8-1b04c3d5d2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461730} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb13a67-84ad-557f-aeee-c7fd80a61c4f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "502954f5-6773-593e-9d60-d2c07c09bac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115276" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero" :topic/_kno