e "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461735} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6a8841-0712-5636-aeca-0549d0f059b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73e30afc-d20e-5512-ba6d-f275ac7bcd6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461735} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6025f86-40f6-5cdc-a65f-78f6bb489b93" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd35b4dd-cb0b-59b9-9a08-35fd436a6a59" :question/math-academy-id "q-115278" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13445-0218-5950-967e-a68bcfec32c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252 :topic/id #uuid "8c876864-a853-56d6-a887-91d7f8f1cf14" :topic/math-academy-id 862 :topic/title "Zero, Square, Diagonal and Identity Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66d0ae76-bc3f-5fc2-ab45-3c327cc7dc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461737 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186568891 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250432} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} {:answer/id #uuid "cf878234-afe8-5395-85e8-d9b0f6944666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66d0ae76-bc3f-5fc2-ab45-3c327cc7dc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461737 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186568891 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250432} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db57aa6-14c2-54c7-b692-04ab1d30d57d" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type